1 NAME Anders /Mårtensson/
2 GIVN Anders
2 SURN Mårtensson
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 6 SEP 1820
1 DEAT
2 DATE 29 APR 1868
1 BURI
2 DATE 3 MAY 1868
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Birger Sjöström writes: "Anders Mårtensson, born 1820. He had moved into their new farm in 1850. Here he and his wife Elizabeth Mary worked on. She was generally called for Lisa Maja. Anders turned out to be a good
2 CONC  farmer and he spared no forces. He cultivated a lot of land and crops, and built several outbuildings. In addition, he set up several kilometers of fence and built many barns out in the suburbs. Much of the feed for the animals w
2 CONC ere taken from outlying lands and marshes.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Nine children were born between 1849 and 1864. Two of the children died early but the other survived. When everything looked good they suffered a hard fate. In the years 1866-1869 were years of misery and famine. These famine aff
2 CONC ected Åsjön with their poor soils more than others villages. And harder sufferings would come. A few years earlier, Anders became afflicted with the dreaded disease tuberculosis. Here in the countryside they said consumption. The 
2 CONC last years Anders lived, he wandered under constant coughing. He still did not stop working but continued with hard work to improve their farming. This was not sustainable in the long run and the disease broke him in the end. On A
2 CONC pril 29, 1868 Anders dies [of bristsjukdom Bröstsjukdom (tuberculosis, TB)] and left behind his wife Elisabet Maria (Lisa Maja) and seven children." After his death a newspaper notice announces the bankruptcy of his estate ("Post 
2 CONC och inrikes tidningar" newspaper, 1868-10-06).
2 CONT
2 CONT (Above from "A History of the Family Mårtensson in Åsjön," by Birger Sjöström (2015), https://www.familysearch.org/memories/memory/194212734 )
2 CONT
2 CONT This all at the height of the famine in northern Sweden between 1866 and 1868, caused by crop failures from harsh winters and extreme summer rain. My great-aunt Mayme (Anderson) Howe, granddaughter of Anders Mårtensson, told me he
2 CONC r father, Anders Andersson was starving and had to eat the inner bark of trees and boil shoe leather to feed themselves. In an article "Bark Bread, Lichen Gruel and Worn Out Shoes" in the proceedings "Food from Nature: Attitudes, 
2 CONC Strategies and Culinary Practices" (Uppsula, 2000), Lars-Olov Sjöstrom mentions:
2 CONT
2 CONT "During the 1860s Norrland was hit by crop failures and economic depression. From 1862 there were many years of crop failures. The bad years for agriculture culminated in 1867-68 when many people starved. During these years the pe
2 CONC ople tried many new foods." To eat boot leather for eating, carve into pieces and boil them to remove most of the tar and other treatments. Then fry like a beef steak. The residue tar still burned in the throat when eaten. To eat 
2 CONC pine bark (Pinus Silvenstris), peel the white inner part of the bark. Remove alkali by rinsing, then dry, grind, and mix with flour. Prepare as bread or gruel. More details are in Sjöstrom's article in the proceedings downloadable
2 CONC  on Internet as a PDF file, currently at https://kgaa.bokorder.se/sv-se/article/2890/food-from-nature
2 CONT
2 CONT Residence: Åsjön, Bygdeå parish, Västerbotten, Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders was born 1820 in Åsjön. Åsjön is about 4 km. east of Åkullsjön, as you follow a stream to a tiny lake.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shown with parents: the kids Anders, Pehr, Maria, Charlotta, Brita, Ingrid, Mag. + Eva, shown on A1:11A.199 (1838-41). Shown with widowed father, Mårten Andersson b. 1789, at his farm as well as his own now with wife + family at A
2 CONC 1:14B.482: from 1820 to his death in 1868.
2 CONT Anders' marriage to Great Grandma Johansdotter-Burman: Mårtensson, Anders. Bondeson Åsjön
2 CONT ... 20 June1848 ... Johansdotter-Strid, Lisa Maja .Bondedotter, Åsjön.
2 CONT Anders died in 1868 at Åsjön, Bygdeå, Västerbotten död bristsjukdom (died from "deficiency disease").
2 CONT
2 CONT Anders brother, Per Mårtensson, was sentenced (dömd) in 1867 to 6 months hard work for counterfeiting (förfalskning). A cousin, Esther Lundströmwrote a book about Per Mårtensson, according to a post by another cousin, Elisabeth Jo
2 CONC hansson "Eljo", 10 Feb 2003 (probably on Anbytarforum, forum.rotter.se, but I cannot find the post--it may be too old or archived).
2 CONT
2 CONT Sources:
2 CONT Birger Sjöström, A History of the Family Mårtensson in Åsjön," (2015) on familysearch memories.
2 CONT Kråken 24 database, https://kråken.se/ person 130549
